<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bamar people</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Burmans, Bamar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Bamar, or Burmans, are the largest ethnic group of Myanmar, forming about two-thirds of its population and living mainly in the central Irrawaddy valley. Speakers of Burmese, a Sino-Tibetan language, they moved south into the dry zone of central Myanmar in the ninth century and founded the Pagan Kingdom, and most of the later Burmese kingdoms were Bamar-led states. Most Bamar are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada</a> Buddhists.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bamar-people.jpg" alt="Bamar people" width="320" height="245" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Upper_class_Burmese_couple_in_the_1890s.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>The Bamar's northern origins are suggested by the distribution of the Burmish languages in the north of the country and by the Burmese word taung, which means both south and mountain, implying that their ancestors once lived north of the mountains.</p><p>Bamar settlers gradually moved into the fertile Irrawaddy and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salween">Salween</a> valleys, which were home to Pyu city-states, and founded the outpost of Pagan. In the 1050s and 1060s King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anawrahta">Anawrahta</a> founded the Pagan Empire, which for the first time united the Irrawaddy valley and its periphery under one polity. By the 1100s Burmese language and culture dominated the upper Irrawaddy valley, and conventional chronicles state that the Pyu were assimilated into the Bamar.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Identity in the pre-colonial era was fluid, marked by patron-client relationships, religion and regional origins, and many non-Bamar adopted a Bamar identity. Between the 1500s and 1800s the notion widened through intermarriage and changes of self-identification, especially in Mon- and Shan-speaking regions. With the Toungoo Empire in the 1500s the Bamar began to populate the lower Irrawaddy valley. For 1,100 years the dry zone of Upper Myanmar was home to a series of Burmese royal capitals, until the British annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/upper-burma">Upper Burma</a> in 1885.</p><p>After the British annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lower-burma">Lower Burma</a> in 1852, millions of Bamar from the dry zone resettled in the Irrawaddy delta between 1858 and 1941, drawn by colonial authorities eager to expand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> cultivation. In the early 1900s a narrower Bamar nationalism arose in response to British rule, partly because of the immigration of people from other parts of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-india">British India</a>. The Dobama Asiayone had emerged by 1930, and independence leaders such as <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/u-nu">U Nu</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aung-san">Aung San</a> began their careers in it.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Bamar identity is generally distinguished by the Burmese language and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theravada-buddhism">Theravada Buddhism</a>. Burmese is a Sino-Tibetan language with a literary tradition, and the oldest surviving written Burmese document is the Myazedi inscription, dated to 1113. Most Bamar combine Theravada Buddhism with the veneration of spirits called nat, which pre-dates <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buddhism">Buddhism</a>. Village life centres on Buddhist monasteries called kyaung, and a household shrine holds a coconut symbolising the guardian nat Mahagiri.</p><p>The Bamar have a single personal name and no surnames, and a common scheme uses the child's day of birth to choose the first letter. They customarily recognise Twelve Auspicious Rites, including the shinbyu ordination of boys. White <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> is the staple of the diet, and pickled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tea">tea</a> leaves, lahpet, play a role in ritual.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bamar people” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The <b>Bamar</b>, or Burmans, of the central Irrawaddy valley form about two-thirds of the population; the Shan, Karen, Rakhine, Mon, Chin, Kachin and many smaller groups live mostly in the hills and borderlands that ring the plains.</blockquote><a href="/empire/myanmar">Myanmar · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Pagan grew out of the migration of Burman, or <b>Bamar</b>, peoples from the north into the dry zone of central Myanmar during the ninth century, after the Pyu city-states that had dominated the region were weakened, partly by raids from the Yunnan kingdom of Nanzhao.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pagan-kingdom">Pagan Kingdom · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The raids weakened the states and let the <b>Burmans</b> move in; the chronicles date the founding of fortified Pagan to 849, but its oldest dated walls belong to 980 and its main walls to about 1020.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pyu-city-states">Pyu city-states · Military</a></li></ul></section>
